From: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:15:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Conf: Free a dropped config instead of finishing reconfiguration

Every config arms the same obstacles_cleared callback, and the callback
assumes that only old_config can lose its last reference. A queued config
that is replaced by a newer one ("Queueing new configuration, ignoring the
one already queued"), or one refused during shutdown, loses its last
reference too. Its callback then ran config_done() while the real old
config was still held by a running show route. When that show route
finished, the old config's callback ran outside any reconfiguration and
bird died on "Assertion 'configuring' failed", or the next configure freed
the still referenced old config and died on the obstacle count assertion.

Free such a config in the callback instead; it was leaked before.

Reproduced with 8 well-behaved clients that run configure or show route
and read every reply to its end.
---
--- a/conf/conf.c
+++ b/conf/conf.c
@@ -81,9 +81,22 @@ int undo_available;			/* Undo was not re
 /* Note that both shutting_down and undo_available are related to requests, not processing */
 
 static void
-config_obstacles_cleared(struct callback *cb UNUSED)
+config_obstacles_cleared(struct callback *cb)
 {
   ASSERT_DIE(birdloop_inside(&main_birdloop));
+
+  /*
+   * A config that never became active lost its last reference: a queued
+   * config replaced by a newer one, or one refused during shutdown. It is
+   * not the one being replaced, so it must not finish the reconfiguration.
+   */
+  struct config *c = SKIP_BACK(struct config, obstacles_cleared, cb);
+  if (c != old_config)
+  {
+    config_free(c);
+    return;
+  }
+
   ASSERT_DIE(configuring);
 
   config_done();
